# 23. Why this becomes extremely cheap

The architecture shifts cost from:

[
C \propto \text{number of reads}
]

toward:

[
C \propto \text{number of meaningful changes}
]

Suppose one scholarly synthesis costs enormous compute to create.

You pay once:

```text
research
LLMs
review
compilation
```

Then one million people/agents can read:

```text
the same immutable bytes
```

mostly through CDN caches.

This is basically perfect for scholarship because scholarly content changes comparatively slowly but can be read indefinitely.

The performance doctrine phrases it extremely well:

> scholarly work happens asynchronously once; reading it should be nearly free forever.

